says what this installation can do with one. For delegates the list is what
they advertise and may be incomplete; ob kind check gives the authoritative
answer for one kind.

Usage:
  ob kind list [flags]

Examples:
  ob kind list
  ob kind list --role invoke

Flags:
  -F, --format string   output format: text, json (default "text")
  -h, --help            help for list
      --role string     only kinds this ob can handle for: invoke, inspect, or synthesize
Say whether this ob can handle an exact kind, for one kind of work (--role)
or for each. Kinds are compared exactly: example.openapi@1 and
example.openapi@2 are unrelated kinds. To see which handler ob would use,
and why, use ob delegate resolve.

Exit status: 0 this ob can handle it (for --role, or for at least one kind of
work); 1 it cannot.

Usage:
  ob kind check <kind> [flags]

Examples:
  ob kind check example.openapi@1
  ob kind check acme.billing-rpc@1 --role invoke

Flags:
  -F, --format string   output format: text, json (default "text")
  -h, --help            help for check
      --role string     the kind of work: invoke, inspect, or synthesize
List the roles ob accepts delegates for, with the operations a delegate
must offer for each.

Usage:
  ob delegate roles [flags]

Examples:
  ob delegate roles

Flags:
  -F, --format string   output format: text, json (default "text")
  -h, --help            help for roles
Register another tool as a delegate for one or more roles. <delegate-obi>
is the tool's OBI (a path, - for stdin, or a URL); ob keeps a copy, checks
that it offers the operations each role needs, and gives the registration an
ID. --preference ROLE=N sets how strongly ob prefers it among delegates for
that role; higher wins.

Usage:
  ob delegate add <delegate-obi> --role <role> [flags]

Examples:
  ob delegate add ./acme-rpc.obi.json --role invoke --preference invoke=10
  ob fetch https://tools.example.com | ob delegate add - --role inspect --role synthesize

Flags:
  -F, --format string            output format: text, json (default "text")
  -h, --help                     help for add
      --preference stringArray   ROLE=NUMBER; higher is preferred (repeatable)
      --role stringArray         a role to register for: invoke, inspect, or synthesize (repeatable)
Change a registered delegate: replace its OBI with --obi, change its roles,
or set its preference for a role. Only the flags you give change anything.
--unset preference.ROLE removes a preference.

Usage:
  ob delegate set <id> [flags]

Examples:
  ob delegate set d_91c2 --preference invoke=20
  ob delegate set d_91c2 --add-role synthesize
  ob delegate set d_91c2 --unset preference.invoke

Flags:
      --add-role stringArray      register it for another role (repeatable)
  -h, --help                      help for set
      --obi string                replace the delegate's OBI: a path, - for stdin, or a URL
      --preference stringArray    ROLE=NUMBER; higher is preferred (repeatable)
      --remove-role stringArray   stop using it for a role (repeatable)
      --unset stringArray         remove a preference: preference.ROLE
Remove a registered delegate. ob stops handing it work and deletes its copy of the delegate's OBI.

Usage:
  ob delegate remove <id> [flags]

Examples:
  ob delegate remove d_7f3a

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for remove
List registered delegates, their roles, and their preferences.

Usage:
  ob delegate list [flags]

Examples:
  ob delegate list
  ob delegate list --role invoke

Flags:
  -F, --format string   output format: text, json (default "text")
  -h, --help            help for list
      --role string     only delegates for this role
Show a registered delegate: its roles, its preference for each, and the operations it offers for each.

Usage:
  ob delegate show <id> [flags]

Examples:
  ob delegate show d_91c2

Flags:
  -F, --format string   output format: text, json (default "text")
  -h, --help            help for show
Explain which handler ob would use for a kind of work on an exact kind: a
built-in handler or a delegate, and why. ob kind check answers only whether
this ob can handle the kind.

Usage:
  ob delegate resolve --role <role> --kind <kind> [flags]

Examples:
  ob delegate resolve --role invoke --kind acme.billing-rpc@1

Flags:
  -F, --format string   output format: text, json (default "text")
  -h, --help            help for resolve
      --kind string     the exact kind (required)
      --role string     the kind of work: invoke, inspect, or synthesize (required)
Generate the autocompletion script for the bash shell.

This script depends on the 'bash-completion' package.
If it is not installed already, you can install it via your OS's package manager.

To load completions in your current shell session:

	source <(ob completion bash)

To load completions for every new session, execute once:

#### Linux:

	ob completion bash > /etc/bash_completion.d/ob

#### macOS:

	ob completion bash > $(brew --prefix)/etc/bash_completion.d/ob

You will need to start a new shell for this setup to take effect.

Usage:
  ob completion bash

Flags:
  -h, --help              help for bash
      --no-descriptions   disable completion descriptions
Generate the autocompletion script for the zsh shell.

If shell completion is not already enabled in your environment you will need
to enable it.  You can execute the following once:

	echo "autoload -U compinit; compinit" >> ~/.zshrc

To load completions in your current shell session:

	source <(ob completion zsh)

To load completions for every new session, execute once:

#### Linux:

	ob completion zsh > "${fpath[1]}/_ob"

#### macOS:

	ob completion zsh > $(brew --prefix)/share/zsh/site-functions/_ob

You will need to start a new shell for this setup to take effect.

Usage:
  ob completion zsh [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help              help for zsh
      --no-descriptions   disable completion descriptions
Generate the autocompletion script for the fish shell.

To load completions in your current shell session:

	ob completion fish | source

To load completions for every new session, execute once:

	ob completion fish > ~/.config/fish/completions/ob.fish

You will need to start a new shell for this setup to take effect.

Usage:
  ob completion fish [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help              help for fish
      --no-descriptions   disable completion descriptions
Generate the autocompletion script for powershell.

